{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented a self-contained scout/confirm controller in [optimal.py](/inspire/hdd/global_user/gongjingjing-25039/sywang/jkcai/FlowEvo/flow_autotts/controllers/optimal.py). The core idea is: use cheap early previews on 4 roots, prune obvious losers, finish only the strongest survivors, and spend higher-beta slack on evidence-driven backward refinement plus late confirmations. This replaces the template’s “finish roots blindly, then maybe refine” pattern with preview-gated allocation.\n\nI inspected `flow_tts_controller_implementation_spec.md`, the injected context pack, `flow_autotts/controllers/optimal.py`, and `flow_autotts/controllers/baselines.py`. The context pack reports no prior rounds, no prior frontier rows, no prior snapshots, and no action-history summaries, so the only concrete rows available were the injected ODE baseline targets:\n- `beta=0.00`: reward `0.676462`, mean NFE `8.0`, but locked target is `10`\n- `beta=0.25`: reward `0.802083`, mean NFE `20.0`\n- `beta=0.50`: reward `0.836650`, mean NFE `36.0`\n- `beta=0.75`: reward `0.843990`, mean NFE `48.0`\n- `beta=1.00`: reward `0.847290`, mean NFE `64.0`\n\nBecause there was no prior proposal history, I could not identify any beta that already beat or lost to ODE, and I could not compare the last two proposal snapshots. I therefore targeted the most likely generic weakness in the template: low/mid beta waste from fully completing branches before gathering evidence, plus high-beta underuse if slack is not converted into useful late confirmation.\n\nThe exact beta-conditioned schedule is monotonic by construction:\n- `beta=0.00`: target `10`; `4` roots; scout at step `1`; keep `2` scout previews; finish `0`; refine `0`.\n- `beta=0.25`: target `20`; `4` roots; scout at step `1`; keep `2`; finish top `1`; late confirmations `2`; refine `0`.\n- `beta=0.50`: target `36`; `4` roots; scout at step `2`; keep `2`; finish top `2`; one backward child from a strong-but-uncertain top anchor; backtrack `1` step.\n- `beta=0.75`: target `48`; `4` roots; scout at step `2`; keep `3`; finish top `3`; up to `2` mixed-noise children; backtrack `2` steps; more conservative pruning and more late confirmations.\n- `beta=1.00`: target `64`; `4` roots; keep all `4` scouts alive initially; finish top `4`; up to `3` mixed-noise children from top anchors; backtrack `3` steps; extra slack can trigger bounded late refinement to stay near the band.\n\nWhat is behaviorally new versus the template and baselines:\n- Early preview-gated survivor selection instead of full completion first.\n- Score-gap and uncertainty gating for whether backward refinement is justified.\n- Anchor allocation for children is selective, not uniform.\n- High-beta slack is spent on bounded late mixed-noise confirmation/refinement, not on blind full branches.\n- Pruning is gentler at high beta to preserve diversity.\n\nChecks I ran:\n- `python -m py_compile flow_autotts/controllers/optimal.py`\n- A tiny fake-env dry run over `beta in {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0}`. Realized NFE there was `10, 18, 32, 45, 60`, which stays inside the injected bands.\n\nTarget-band reflection and expected utilization:\n- `beta=0.00`, ceiling/band `[10,10]`: expected to hit `10` exactly. Underuse is not allowed here, so I forced a 4-root, 1-step scout plus previews and no refinement.\n- `beta=0.25`, ceiling/band `[18,20]`: expected around `18-20`. Any slack is meant to be small and justified; I kept this regime conservative because low-beta extra branching can lose to ODE if evidence is weak.\n- `beta=0.50`, ceiling/band `[32,36]`: expected around `32-36`. I adjusted this regime upward with 2 finished survivors plus one selective child, because underuse here looked more likely to be conservative allocation than justified stopping.\n- `beta=0.75`, ceiling/band `[44,48]`: expected around `44-48`. I added a third survivor and a second child so this regime spends near the ceiling without collapsing too early.\n- `beta=1.00`, ceiling/band `[60,64]`: expected around `60-64`. I explicitly revised the policy after reflection to avoid unnecessary underuse by keeping all 4 scouts alive and allowing slack-triggered late mixed-noise refinement.\n\nMain expected improvement: `beta=0.25` and `beta=0.50` should be less vulnerable to wasting NFE on weak full completions, while `beta=0.75` and `beta=1.0` should use the available budget more effectively through selective confirmation rather than blind breadth. Main risk: early PickScore previews may still be noisy enough that scout pruning drops a late-blooming branch, especially around `beta=0.25`."}}
